WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.120
Hi, I'm Ryan Sumner and I am a Chief Network Architect with IBM Cloud.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.900
Do you know what "VPC" means?

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.700
- and why it even matters to you in your public cloud journey?

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.030
Well, today I'm going break it down.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:13.680
VPC, or "Virtual Private Cloud",

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.180
is a public cloud capability

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.040
that provides you the ability to define and then control

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.980
isolated virtual networks

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.100
and then deploy cloud resources into those networks.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.100
So, what is a virtual network?

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.760
Well, to help you understand that,

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.180
let me first help you understand

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.130
how we would deploy networks

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.100
in a standard public cloud.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.880
An administrator is going to find a backbone,
now that backbone is going to carry all the

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.340
traffic in that cloud.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:48.310
There's going to be some segmentation on that
backbone to create a separation between one

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:54.900
client and another client. Or let's even say
separation between one application and another

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.310
application within that same client.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:01.420
So, now that we have that segmentation, we
need a network function that will actually

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:05.659
allow us to have communication between those
segments.

00:01:05.659 --> 00:01:08.950
A network function we're going to call router.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:17.090
So, now that we have communication between
these segments I might choose to say I don't

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:20.430
want traffic to flow between this segment
and that segment.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:24.610
Let's say because this is customer A and this
is customer B.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:29.840
So, now I have a firewall function that provides
us with filtering capabilities.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.021
So, now I have my cloud defined, it's completely
isolated, it's not connected to the rest of

00:01:35.021 --> 00:01:39.710
the world, but I need internet connectivity
because I'm hosting web application here.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:43.340
So, now I need a network function that can
provide me with NAT-ing.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:49.249
In addition, I need to extend my enterprise.
Or I have applications here that need to communicate

00:01:49.249 --> 00:01:52.909
and get data from my enterprise on site.

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:56.969
So, I'm going to build a VPN function.

00:01:56.969 --> 00:02:02.009
So, in a traditional cloud environment most
of all these network functions are actually

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:06.399
done with appliances. There done with appliances
that require infrastructure administrators,

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:11.860
or network administrators to log into them
using proprietary interface to define all

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:15.920
these flows and controls.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.600
If you look at virtual networking, however
we introduce all of these capabilities as

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:24.080
a service. For which in case, we introduce
all these capabilities to the user where they

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.170
can now create these functions and create
this isolation and the segmentation with a

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.880
UI or CLI or API.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:40.000
So, they might be able to say I want four
of these networks, and I want to find my own

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.680
custom segmentation for this application for
that application.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:50.140
Now I want to have connectivity to my enterprise,
I provision a VPN service to get connectivity

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:56.640
to the internet instead of having to configure
NAT-ing I provided, I provision a service

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.000
to be able to be able to give me that ability.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.410
So, now the user has this control and they
didn't need to know any proprietary interfaces

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:09.120
to make these connections are defined these
flows, and since each of these are networks,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.660
they are completely isolated with each other.
They don't have connectivity between each

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.010
network until I say so.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:19.080
So, let's talk about a few the benefits. Now
you understand where the virtual network comes

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:24.960
into play and how that actually contributes
to the private piece of V. P. C.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:30.700
So, some of the benefits are because now I
can get all these all these functions and

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:37.850
I have isolation built in I have some security
aspects.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:42.570
In addition, since these are not appliances
and actually provided has a capability of

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:48.130
the cloud, I can do all of this at scale.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:56.100
Again, developers need things that are customizable
right. So, the aspect of being able to find

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.430
the segmentation and say I want four, five,
or six, or tear it all down and come back

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:09.590
tomorrow to do it again, I need some aspects
of customizing this environment.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:20.159
In addition, it's flexible enough 
to allow the user to be able to say I need

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:25.219
to be able to add virtual segmentation later
on down the road, or I need connectivity to

00:04:25.219 --> 00:04:27.129
the enterprise down the road.

00:04:27.129 --> 00:04:35.229
So, these two attributes here actually allow
the developers to become more agile which

00:04:35.229 --> 00:04:40.580
hopefully is going to save you some money.
So, now you understand what virtual private

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.889
cloud is and why it matters to you.

00:04:42.889 --> 00:04:44.870
We hope you like this video on VPC.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:49.530
If you did, or have any questions, please
comment below and don't forget to like and

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.030
subscribe.

